{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.72,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U4", "U5", "U7", "U10"],
      "pins": ["U4.18", "U4.19", "U4.20", "U4.21", "U4.22", "U4.16", "U5.5", "U7.5", "U10.5", "U5.6", "U7.6", "U10.6"],
      "nets": ["NET0011 (SCK)", "NET0015 (CS)", "NET0002 (CS)", "NET0001 (CS)", "NET0016 (SO)"],
      "obs": "On the Thermocouple sheet the shared SPI clock net NET0011 is the pin-5 (SCK) net of all three MAX31855s (U5/U7/U10), and the three chip-select nets are NET0015 (U10 pin6), NET0002 (U7 pin6) and NET0001 (U5 pin6). On the MCU sheet these same nets land on the wrong ESP32-C3 GPIOs: NET0011/SCK -> U4 pin18 = GPIO04; NET0015/CS -> pin19 = GPIO03; NET0002/CS -> pin20 = GPIO05; NET0001/CS -> pin21 = GPIO06; and pin22 = GPIO07 is left unconnected. Only SO (NET0016 -> pin16 = GPIO10) matches the spec.",
      "exp": "The specification fixes the SPI/CS assignment: 'SPI2 on GPIO5/GPIO10, CS pins GPIO4/6/7' and 'tied to the ESP32 (U1 GPIO5/SCK, GPIO10/SO)' with 'separate nets CS0/CS1/CS2, driven by ESP32 GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7 respectively'. So SCK must be on GPIO5 and the three CS lines on GPIO4/6/7.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "SPI2 on GPIO5/GPIO10, CS pins GPIO4/6/7 (firmware/src/bin/main.rs)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "SCK (pin 5) and SO (pin 7...) are common nets (SCK, SO) tied to all three ICs and to the ESP32 (U1 GPIO5/SCK, GPIO10/SO). Each chip has its own independent chip-select... driven by ESP32 GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7 respectively."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch: U4 pin18 'GPIO04/ADC1_CH4' -> NET0011; pin19 'GPIO03' -> NET0015; pin20 'GPIO05' -> NET0002; pin21 'GPIO06' -> NET0001; pin22 'GPIO07' no net."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "thermocouples.kicad_sch: U5/U7/U10 pin5 SCK all -> NET0011; pin6 CS -> NET0001/NET0002/NET0015."}
      ],
      "impact": "The firmware's SPI2 peripheral is configured on GPIO5 (SCK) and GPIO4/6/7 (CS), but the board routes SCK to GPIO4, a CS to GPIO3, a CS to GPIO5 and leaves GPIO7 (CS2) floating. The MCU would clock the bus on a CS pin and toggle CS on the SCK/USB pins, so none of the three thermocouple channels can be read correctly; all zones would report faults and the heater control loop would never see valid temperatures.",
      "confidence": 0.72
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The power, heater, USB-C and buck sections match the spec, but the thermocouple SPI bus is misrouted on the MCU: SCK is tied to GPIO4 (spec: GPIO5), the chip-selects to GPIO3/5/6 (spec: GPIO4/6/7), and GPIO7 is left unconnected, contradicting the firmware's fixed GPIO5/GPIO10 + GPIO4/6/7 assignment and breaking all three thermocouple reads."
}

